lo":-5.813955,"d":1063,"ph":"+212 5 39 94 74 36","ad":"25 Rue du Mexique","wifi":1},{"n":"CIH Bank","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":35.780497,"lo":-5.814352,"d":1065,"ad":"Avenue Belgique"},{"n":"Post office","g":"essentials","k":"post_office","la":35.781008,"lo":-5.815292,"d":1078},{"n":"Lalla Abla Mosque","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.78946,"lo":-5.807,"d":162,"ds":"The Lalla Abla Mosque (Arabic: مسجد للا عبلة), also known as the Port Mosque, is a mosque in Tangier, Morocco, completed in 2017 and dedicated by King Mohammed VI in July 2018. It replaced a smaller mosque at a nearby location, also known as the Port Mosque.","wpu":"Lalla Abla Mosque","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Lalla_Abla_Mosque.jpg/960px-Lalla_Abla_Mosque.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Abdelhafid Palace","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.78557,"lo":-5.82153,"d":1338,"ds":"The Abdelhafid Palace or Moulay Hafid Palace is a historic structure at 23, rue Mohammed Ben Abedelouhab in the Hasnouna neighborhood of Tangier, Morocco. It was built in 1912–1913 as the intended main residence of former Sultan Abdelhafid following his abdication, but was never used for that purpose.","wpu":"Abdelhafid Palace","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Morocco_Tangier_Palace_Italy.jpg/960px-Morocco_Tangier_Palace_Italy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Mohammed V Mosque, Tangier","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.781,"lo":-5.8197,"d":1384,"ds":"The Mohammed V Mosque (Arabic: مسجد محمد الخامس) is a large mosque in Tangier, Morocco, completed in 1983. It was erected on the initiative of then Crown Prince Mohammed of Morocco, now King Mohammed VI, and named after his grandfather Mohammed V who had given a historic address in Tangier in 1947.","wpu":"Mohammed V Mosque, Tangier","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%AC%D8%AF_%D9%85%D8%AD%D9%85%D8%AF_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AE%D8%A7%D9%85%D8%B3_%D8%A8%D8%B7%D9%86%D8%AC%D8%A9_02.jpg/960px-%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%AC%D8%AF_%D9%85%D8%AD%D9%85%D8%AF_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AE%D8%A7%D9%85%D8%B3_%D8%A8%D8%B7%D9%86%D8%AC%D8%A9_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Café Hafa","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.7915,"lo":-5.8218,"d":1390,"ds":"Café Hafa (Arabic: مقهى الحافة) is a cafe in Tangier, Morocco, located along the cliff top overlooking the Bay of Tangier. Opened in 1921, the cafe still maintains its original decor with multiple levels of terrace seating, facing the Strait of Gibraltar with Tarifa, Spain visible in the distance.","wpu":"Café Hafa","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/39/Caf%C3%A9_Hafa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"French Church of Tangier","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.7747,"lo":-5.8071,"d":1478,"ds":"The French Church of Tangier, formally the Church of Our Lady of the Assumption and of Saint Joan of Arc (French: Église Notre-Dame-de-l'Assomption et Sainte-Jeanne-d'Arc), also known as Sainte-Marie-Sainte-Jeanne-d'Arc, shorthand Sainte-Marie-Jeanne, or the Église des Sables (lit. 'Sands Church'), is a parish church in Tangier, Morocco.","wpu":"French Church of Tangier","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Morocco_Tangier_French_Church.jpg/960px-Morocco_Tangier_French_Church.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"St Andrew's Church, Tangier","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.784722,"lo":-5.823056,"d":1493,"ds":"The Church of Saint Andrew is an Anglican church in Tangier, Morocco. Consecrated in 1905, the church is within the Archdeaconry of Gibraltar. The building is constructed in a Moorish architectural style.","wpu":"St Andrew's Church, Tangier","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Church_of_St_Andrew%2C_Tangier.jpg/960px-Church_of_St_Andrew%2C_Tangier.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Bay of Tangier","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.791111,"lo":-5.782778,"d":2212,"ds":"The Bay of Tangier (Arabic: خليج طنجة) is a bay around Tangier on the Mediterranean in northern Morocco. It forms the body of water between the port and Cap Malabata in a semi circular shape. Avenue d'Espagne runs along the bay and is known for its hotels and large modern establishments.","wpu":"Bay of Tangier","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Tangiermorocconasa.jpg/960px-Tangiermorocconasa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Cape Malabata","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.816667,"lo":-5.748333,"d":6176,"ds":"Cape Malabata (Arabic: رأس ملباطا, Ras Malabata, or Rās al-Manār, \"Lighthouse Cape\"; French: Cap Malabata; Spanish: Punta Malabata) is a cape located about 6 miles (10 km) east of central Tangier, Morocco, facing the Strait of Gibraltar. The cape features a lighthouse and a medieval-style castle that was built in early 20th century.","wpu":"Cape Malabata","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Cape_Malabata_Lighthouse.jpg/960px-Cape_Malabata_Lighthouse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Perdicaris Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.788,"lo":-5.883,"d":6855,"ds":"The Perdicaris Park, also known as the Rmilat Forest and formerly as Villa Aidonia or Place of the Nightingales, is a public park covering 70 hectares in the Rmilat neighborhood of Tangier, Morocco. It is located on the northern slopes of the Jebel Kebir hill facing the Strait of Gibraltar, between the Cape Spartel reserve to its west and the Jebel Kebir Royal Palace to its east.","wpu":"Perdicaris Park","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Perdicares_palace_Tangier.jpg/960px-Perdicares_palace_Tangier.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Tangier, also called Tangiers, is a city in northwestern Morocco, on the coasts of the Mediterranean Sea and the Atlantic Ocean. The city is the capital of the Tangier-Tetouan-Al Hoceima region, as well as the Tangier-Assilah Prefecture of Morocco. Many civilisations and cultures have influenced the history of Tangier, starting from before the 10th century BCE. Originating as a strategic Phoenician town and trading centre, Tangier has been a nexus for many cultures.","wpu":"Tangier","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a4/Tanger_cor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}